WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:07.550
This video was made possible by Assassin’s
Creed Origins, which is out today.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.240
When we think of ancient Egypt, most imagine
sand swept plains with giant pyramids jousting

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.460
up from a barren desert, juxtaposed next to
the lush meandering Nile spilling nutrients

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.619
to into the surrounding floodplains to feed
this burgeoning empire.

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:24.849
But all we have for reference for this vision
of the past are the ancient monuments and

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:29.669
artifacts that remain and ancient hieroglyphs,
that puzzled linguists for centuries.

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:34.610
The ways of this ancient world is shrouded
in mystery, so when I was invited out by Ubisoft

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:39.070
to get a private tour of the British Museum,
speak with historians and get hands on experience

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:43.920
with their 3D explorable vision of ancient
Egypt in Assassin's Creed Origins to learn

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.090
more, I jumped at the opportunity.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.039
Today we are going to explore this ancient
world and try to uncover one it’s greatest

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:55.030
secrets, how a civilisation without power
machinery managed to build those gigantic

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.670
godly structure.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:04.120
In our modern world, these structures may
seem to some as just huge piles of rock.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:08.140
With modern machinery, like cranes, it may
be easy to take for granted the monumental

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.340
task of moving and lifting this much material.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.479
So let’s put this problem in context to
understand why it has baffled historians for

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:16.479
centuries.

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:19.990
The largest of the Great Pyramids, Pyramid
Khufu, was the tallest man made structure

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.820
on earth for almost four thousands years.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:28.409
Pyramid Khufu contains over 2.3 million limestone
blocks, each weighing an average of 2.5 tonnes,

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:33.090
which form the interior structure of the pyramid
and an additional 200,000 blocks were used

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.690
for the smooth white stone casing, which now
only exists on the upper cap.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:41.890
but the Pyramids aren’t simply giant piles
of limestone arranged nicely, Pyramid Khufu,

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.189
incredibly, contains massive interior chambers,
like the king’s chamber, which is supported

00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:50.619
by massive granite beams, with the heaviest
weighing about 40 tonnes, that’s about the

00:01:50.619 --> 00:01:54.700
same as the maximum allowable weight of a
fully laden articulated truck.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.360
These stones are located between 43 metres
and 65 metres up the pyramid.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.320
Just transporting the massive stones to the
site from the quarries was a massive undertaking,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.040
but other civilisations that predate the Egyptians
have achieved similar feats, like stonehenge,

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.360
or the even more ancient Irish building of
Newgrange.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.530
The limestone for the Pyramids was quarried
nearby, much of it quarried directly next

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:21.310
to the pyramids, while the granite was likely
taken from Aswan and transported to the site

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.360
on boats.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.580
We know from hieroglyphs that the egyptians
used wooden sleds to transport heavy objects,

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.160
while workers lubricated it’s movement with
water.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.490
This was simply a problem of manpower, which
the wealthy Egyptian empire was not short

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:34.490
on.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:38.390
What has fascinated me is the logistic challenge
of building a structure like this, the sequence

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:42.780
of events and mechanisms that these ancient
engineers used to lift these immense weights

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.130
into place are a mystery, a mystery that many
have tried to solve, but many theories come

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.160
up short.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.350
To solve this riddle, let’s start with what
we do know.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.150
First things first, unless the Pyramids were
truly built by aliens with anti-gravity machines,

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.920
the pyramids were built from the bottom.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.310
Next, because of the precise geometric shape
of the pyramids, with each side of the Great

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:07.120
Pyramid being 230.3 metres long, with the
largest difference in lengths, between the

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.150
Northern and Southern edge, at only 4.4 centimetres.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.630
This level of accuracy could only have been
possible if the external casing stones were

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.280
laid first, allowing any deviations to be
caught early and corrected, before the internal

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.620
structure was filled in.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:24.340
This makes any theories for external ramps
or cranes unlikely, as they would have been

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.490
extremely difficult to attach to the smooth
external wall, which was inclined at 52 degrees.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.450
Instead, the most prevalent theory suggests
a ramp on one side of the pyramid that would

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.360
raise with pyramid, but this has it’s limit.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.840
When pulling a weight like this uphill, the
force required is given by this equation.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:46.360
The average mass of blocks was 2.5 tonnes,
or about 24,500 Newton's, there were heavier

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.820
blocks, but these were mainly used in the
lower levels.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:53.320
Let’s set our slope angle alpha, at 7 degrees,
and the coefficient of friction between two

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.930
pieces of wet wood is about 0.1, using these
figures we can calculate the force needed

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:02.530
to pull this weight uphill at 5418 Newtons.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:07.251
Modern safety guidelines say the maximum any
worker should pull is 225 Newtons [1], but

00:04:07.251 --> 00:04:11.690
since this is a prolonged track and stopping
is not an option, most calculate the max each

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:15.780
worker would pull at 150 Newtons or about
15.3 kg.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.220
So that means we would need around 36 workers
would be needed to haul this weight.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.990
This figure is reasonable, but for every additional
degree of slope at least an extra 3 workers

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.140
will be needed, which would soon become extremely
impractical.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.800
The other option, of simply elongating the
ramp and maintaining the angle is completely

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.560
unfeasible, as constructing the ramp would
be bigger project than the great pyramid itself.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:39.450
Like any modern construction project, these
are all logistical issues the Egyptians would

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:40.580
have had to overcome.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.130
This is the core of engineering.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.460
Trying to solve problems with the tools at
hand, and now we have to reverse engineer

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.140
this problem without knowing exactly what
tools the Egyptians had.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.460
One of the most convincing theories I have
seen, from French architect, Jean-Pierre Houdin,

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.510
may hold the truth of the Pyramids construction.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:59.540
Jean-Pierre theorised that this external ramp
was used to construct up to the height of

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.620
the King's Chamber at around 43 metres, allowing
the heaviest granite stones used to be hauled

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.930
up this ramp.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:09.610
Granite has often been used in grand historic
buildings for it’s superior compressive

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:14.090
strength, allowing the buildings to grow in
size, here the granite was tasked with supporting

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:17.110
the immense weight of the pyramid above the
King’s Chamber.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:20.960
At this height the majority of the volume
of the pyramid is completed, but we still

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.980
need a way of transporting those final blocks
into place.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:27.020
This is where the innovative part of Jean-Pierre’s
theory comes in.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:31.750
He proposes that while the first 43 metres
was being constructed, the workers also built

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:36.250
an internal ramp, which would transport material
recycled from the exterior ramp to the top

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.420
of the pyramid.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.930
Now this theory should be easy to prove, the
interior ramp should still exist, but the

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:46.000
Pyramid is a protected structure, we can’t
just take it apart to see what’s inside.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.171
But we do have tools that can peer into the
internal structure without damaging it, one

00:05:50.171 --> 00:05:55.940
team used microgravimetry in 1986 to measure
the density of different sections of the pyramid.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:59.640
This research produced this image image, showing
a strange pattern where the green indicates

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.300
lower density, which closely matches the pattern
of Jean Piere’s predicted internal ramp.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:09.040
I believe this is the most convincing theory
to date, but it is yet to be confirmed or

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.040
disproven.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.650
The mystery of the pyramids construction may
remain a mystery forever, but you can explore

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:17.550
this mysterious world in Assassin’s Creed
Origins, which is out today.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:21.060
I fell in love with the Assassin’s Creed
franchise when I got to explore Florence in

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:22.130
Assassin’s Creed 2.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:27.000
It gave me a unique insight into this distant
world, and I got the exact same feeling exploring

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.610
Egypt in Assassin’s Creed Origins.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.830
They are even including a mode called Discovery
Tour to turn off the hippo violence, and give

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:36.030
you guided tours around this beautiful recreation
of the past.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:39.800
I really enjoyed the game, if it sounds like
something you are into, check it out with

